 the local  company that that you go to  also sometimes like the toilet paper  rolls for businesses are gigantic you  know it just doesn't work at home but  also  the companies doing the the commerce  servicing office buildings  don't have any connection whatsoever to  like cvs or staples or or whatever so  there were really a whole bunch of  things that would have had to change now  would it have been simpler if the toilet  paper manufacturers dealt directly with  everyone i don't know that would have  had its own share of problems  yeah and part of the book is really not  saying direct is the solution as much as  direct as the cornerstone and a  cornerstone really is a very very small  piece of a much larger edifice so the  point of saying we've gotten to this  hyper-intermediated world where  generally speaking the instinct was  always to add additional layers of  middlemen but as you nicely pointed out  earlier there's already a counter  movement underway there's already a  number of people saying to be able to  really get what i want to have my values  match my actions i need to opt out of  the system entirely and so by  documenting both the growth of this  hyper intermediate world and this  revolution well then we can figure out  how to to navigate this territory in  between and the toilet paper is a great  example of it uh so as you're pointing  out the nature of the supply chains had  incredible rigidities which just meant  like they were used to doing one things  and they couldn't easily change right  and oftentimes what we're seeing with  those rigidities the complexity really  accentuates them and it's not about  going direct but having  fewer middlemen and shorter chains can  oftentimes enhance resilience and  enhance flexibility so trying to figure  out where did we add layers  over time without anybody really  understanding well what are the states  of the world  uh where things might go wrong and where  that might create problems or going back  to our earlier conversation where there  might be a lack of accountability  because now there's just no way for us  to actually see through all of the  different layers involved there are some  industries where  there's been a move into a more direct  model one of the ones i think is  interesting is you know for for me the  experience of going to car dealers has  never been good i mean it's just it's  not a pleasant experience very low  uh satisfaction with it for the average  consumer i ordered my last car online  it's a tesla i didn't have to talk to  anybody i picked the features i wanted  there was no negotiation  and then i got a call when the car was  ready and it was a dramatically better  experience now this is all aside from  all the other concerns about elon musk's  tweets all these different things i get  that but as an experience the dealership  is a terrible experience and it seems to  me that it would only make the end  product more expensive one report says  on average people pay about twelve  hundred dollars more for vehicles than  they otherwise would because of the the  dealership experience another one that  as many real estate agents that i have  in my social circle that i think  could be eliminated is the idea that a  realtor  regularly takes you know a slice out of  every real estate transaction can sell  the same house multiple times in many  countries you just deal with a lawyer  and you don't need the entire  infrastructure of the real estate agent  it you could potentially cut out a  middleman can you talk about either of  those industries and or other industries  that have done well at moving to a more  direct model  so both absolutely outstanding examples  i spent a lot of time on residential  real estate and  the  car market fits equally well and i'd  actually done a huge amount of research  and only left it out because i had so  many other great examples so the great  thing about your tesla example is yes it  was a better experience and contrary to  our earlier conversation it was cheaper  you're cutting out an expensive  middleman and you're saving real money  and same thing in residential real  estate we know for your average middle  class family the median family united  states they don't have most of their  money in stocks and bonds  housing is our number one source of  wealth so when real estates come agents  come in and take five percent on average  even now of every single real estate  transaction you are eating directly out  of the bottom line the nest egg of your  typical middle class family in a very  meaningful way now many of the real  estate agents in their defense are also  middle class right i mean there's two  sides to that to be fair  yeah the hard thing with a real estate  agent is there's some really good  research just showing that we have too  many of them so they provide very real  value but it's relatively easy to become  a real estate agent so what we've seen  is there's some great work showing you  know comparing for example minneapolis  and boston and saying look when housing  prices were about the same uh each agent  sold on average like 10 houses a year or  12 houses a year and then suddenly the  boston housing market went crazy so a  decade later what happened  real estate agents in minneapolis were  still selling 10 to 12 houses a year  real estate agents in boston their  income was about the same but they were  suddenly selling on average four to five  houses a year and that's because so the  commissions went up because there were  percentage of sales so so many more  people became real estate agents and  then so what do they all do they send  out like really glossy mailings to  people who already own their homes just  saying like choose me if you're going to  sell so it's not actually against the  real estate agents themselves it's a  system that doesn't serve the agents and  really doesn't serve homeowners right  and one nice thing about both the the  car market and the real estate market is  they are examples  where  there have been state laws across  numerous states  that entrench the current system  so for example if you look at  penetration of electric vehicle sales  one of the  most significant indicators  is do you have state laws that  effectively entrench the the need to use  a car dealership doesn't new jersey have  such a law  yeah and i think so i should double  check but a number of a number of states  do and 10 states still have things  protecting effectively effectively  prohibiting rebates because really for  real estate it's the buy side more than  the sell side where one where a lot of  countries have managed to get rid of the  agent so oftentimes sellers need a  little more handholding but you're also  paying a lot to a buyer where they're  doing their own work on zillow or redfin  but of course what you're really tapping  into there is the mls which is still  controlled by full service real estate  agents and so those are both great  examples where when you really look at  the mechanisms they are layered and a  little complicated but where you see  people who came in originally and  provided value  and then in a post-internet era where  there's so much more ability for  consumers to do things for themselves we  should have had a change in the market  structure but the change has been  relatively modest  relative to the cost savings and the  alternatives that open up and it's  partly because  the  the entrenched middlemen really do a  good job  to  to undermine  the the alternatives that otherwise  would be available both through market  mechanisms and through through legal  mechanisms yeah justifying their own  their own existence essentially uh the  book is direct the rise of the middleman  economy and the power of going to the  source we've been speaking with the  books author catherine judge who teaches  law at columbia law school thank you so  much for your time today i really  appreciate it  thank you for having me
 

